{"text": "Duolingo didn't turn an owl into a mascot. They turned guilt into a growth engine. Because before Duo was internet famous, he was basically the most annoying part of the product. That little green owl showing up like, hey, you forgot your Spanish lesson. Most brands would hide that. Duolingo did the opposite. They took the joke everyone already had in their head and made it the entire personality of the brand. Suddenly, Duo wasn't a logo. He was a character. Petty, needy, unhinged, low-key threatening, and weirdly lovable. That's the genius part. Duolingo didn't force a brand voice onto the internet. They found the emotion people already associated with the app and exaggerated it until it became culture. That's why the TikTok worked. Not because the owl was random, because the owl made the product feel alive. Most brands try to look professional. Duolingo became recognizable. And on social, recognizable beats polished almost every time. That's the whole play. Don't build a mascot. Build a memory people can joke about.", "tokens": [" D", "u", "ol", "ing", "o", " didn", "'", "t", " t", "urn", " an", " o", "w", "l", " into", " a", " m", "as", "c", "ot", ".", " They", " t", "urn", "ed", " gu", "il", "t", " into", " a", " gr", "ow", "th", " en", "g", "ine", ".", " Because", " before", " D", "u", "o", " was", " inter", "n", "et", " f", "am", "ous", ",", " he", " was", " bas", "ically", " the", " most", " an", "n", "oy", "ing", " part", " of", " the", " prod", "uct", ".", " That", " little", " g", "re", "en", " o", "w", "l", " show", "ing", " up", " like", ",", " he", "y", ",", " you", " for", "g", "ot", " your", " S", "p", "an", "ish", " l", "ess", "on", ".", " M", "ost", " b", "ra", "nds", " would", " h", "ide", " that", ".", " D", "u", "ol", "ing", "o", " did", " the", " o", "pp", "os", "ite", ".", " They", " to", "ok", " the", " j", "o", "ke", " every", "one", " al", "re", "ady", " had", " in", " their", " he", "ad", " and", " made", " it", " the", " ent", "i